  "title": "Polaroid SX-70 Sonar OneStep",
  "subtitle": "The camera that measured the world with a chirp — the first consumer SLR to autofocus itself with sonar",
  "tags": ["Camera", "Autofocus", "Sensing", "Spatial Input", "Consumer", "Embedded Computing"],
  "blurb": "The Polaroid SX-70 Sonar OneStep (1978) put a 50 kHz sonar transceiver on the front of the folding SX-70 and made focus a computation the camera did for you. Press the shutter button halfway and a transducer fires four ultrasonic chirps; the echo time gives distance to a chosen target, and a motor drives the lens to the matching position. It was the first consumer SLR with autofocus. The interaction moment is exactly the one we now live inside: the user composes and points, and the *machine* closes the last sensing loop that photography had reserved for the human eye and hand.",
